## Native permissions

Each resource and data-source page lists its required native grants and scope.
Account ownership and role definitions do not themselves grant API access; use
explicit `onpager_role_assignment` resources. The initial credential and authority
must exist before applying the configuration.

Import and refresh require read grants. Identity data sources require tenant
`users.read`, `roles.read` or `groups.read`. Delivery channel metadata requires
`delivery_channels.read`. Team/service reads use `teams.read`/`services.read` in
the applicable team or tenant scope. Public `lookups.read` does not authorize
private user login/email selectors. Dataset content requires the separate
`enrichment_dataset_rows.read` grant.

Bot creation and credential replacement require `bot_credentials.replace` in
addition to `bots.create` or `bots.update`. Voice connections use the corresponding
`voice_provider_credentials.replace` and `voice_providers.*` actions. The built-in
configuration role does not implicitly grant credential replacement; assign those
actions explicitly when automation manages connection secrets.

Role assignment updates require `role_assignments.delete` in the stored source
scope and `role_assignments.create` in the desired destination scope. No separate
update grant exists. Team role defaults require tenant
`team_role_defaults.read` and `team_role_defaults.update` for writes. Their
resource does not require directory or role-picker reads. The permissions data
source needs an authenticated credential; catalog metadata grants no authority.

A read authorization error produces a diagnostic and retains managed resource
state. Use an automation account whose lifetime is independent of teams being
retired: disabling an owning team can disable its service accounts and revoke
their tokens.

## Request timezone

Provider `timezone` sets the IANA `Onpager-Timezone` header for both PAT and
service-account requests. An explicit `UTC` value overrides a user's profile
zone. Omission or `null` sends no header: user requests use the profile/tenant
default; service accounts use UTC. Stored preferences are unchanged.

Empty, unknown or invalid provider values are errors. The API returns 422
`invalid_time_zone` for empty, invalid or repeated headers. Local DST gaps and
ambiguous local timestamps also produce API diagnostics.

The API interprets datetime strings without offsets and serializes responses.
Explicit RFC3339 offsets preserve absolute instants. The provider performs no
zone conversion. Storage, recurrence and source weekdays retain UTC semantics;
date-only values and durations retain their domain meaning.

For resources with local datetime input, private state stores server-confirmed
instants and the original configured text. Computed `request_timezone` makes a
provider-zone change visible in the plan for local inputs. Import adopts explicit
offset timestamps from the API. Explicit-offset configurations do not change
instants when only the provider zone changes.

## Secrets in state

Integration tokens and configured integration/webhook/bot/voice credentials can
persist in Terraform state. Voice callback URLs are sensitive capability values
and also persist when the API returns them. Refresh preserves known write-only values when the API omits
secret material. Resource pages specify secret readback and replacement behavior.
Connection secret-key metadata cannot detect replacement of an existing secret
outside Terraform; configure an explicit replacement to reconcile its value.
Keep credential-bearing state, plan and variable files outside the Git file set;
protect the configured Terraform backend separately.
